Assistant: The error is occurring because the component is trying to use the Node.js `crypto` module, which isn't available in the browser. We need to mock this functionality. Let's create a mock for the crypto-related utilities:

<file path="apps/web/.temp/dessn-component-routes/ServerPage-b7d87317.tsx">
<change>
<description>Add crypto mock and update component to handle server-side dependencies</description>
<content>
import React, { Suspense } from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';

// Mock crypto functionality
const mockCrypto = {
  randomBytes: (size) => {
    const array = new Uint8Array(size);
    crypto.getRandomValues(array);
    return {
      toString: (format) => {
        if (format === 'hex') {
          return Array.from(array)
            .map(b => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0'))
            .join('');
        }
        return array.toString();
      }
    };
  }
};

// Mock the generateNonce function
globalThis.generateNonce = () => {
  return mockCrypto.randomBytes(16).toString('hex');
};

// Mock providers to avoid dependency issues
const MockSessionProvider = ({ children }) => <>{children}</>;
const MockI18nextProvider = ({ children }) => <>{children}</>;
const MockTooltipProvider = ({ children }) => <>{children}</>;
const MockFeatureProvider = ({ children }) => <>{children}</>;
const MockQueryClientProvider = ({ children }) => <>{children}</>;

// Mock any server-side only functionality
if (typeof window !== 'undefined') {
  window.crypto = window.crypto || {
    getRandomValues: function(buf) {
      for (let i = 0; i < buf.length; i++) {
        buf[i] = Math.floor(Math.random() * 256);
      }
      return buf;
    }
  };
}

// Import component directly instead of using dynamic import
import ImportedComponent from '../../app/(use-page-wrapper)/signup/page';

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state, setState] = useParentState({
    params: {
      type: "string",
      value: JSON.stringify({ id: "example" }),
      label: "Params",
    },
    searchParams: {
      type: "string",
      value: JSON.stringify({ query: "test" }),
      label: "Search Params",
    },
  });

  const pageProps = {
    params: JSON.parse(state.params.value),
    searchParams: JSON.parse(state.searchParams.value),
  };

  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
      <ErrorBoundary>
        <MockSessionProvider>
          <MockI18nextProvider>
            <MockQueryClientProvider>
              <MockTooltipProvider>
                <MockFeatureProvider>
                  <ImportedComponent {...pageProps} />
                </MockFeatureProvider>
              </MockTooltipProvider>
            </MockQueryClientProvider>
          </MockI18nextProvider>
        </MockSessionProvider>
      </ErrorBoundary>
    </Suspense>
  );
}

// Simple Error Boundary component
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { hasError: false };
  }

  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) {
    return { hasError: true };
  }

  componentDidCatch(error, errorInfo) {
    console.error('Component Error:', error, errorInfo);
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) {
      return <div>Something went wrong loading the component.</div>;
    }

    return this.props.children;
  }
}

// Mock any other Node.js specific modules that might be used
if (typeof window !== 'undefined') {
  window.crypto = window.crypto || {
    getRandomValues: function(buf) {
      for (let i = 0; i < buf.length; i++) {
        buf[i] = Math.floor(Math.random() * 256);
      }
      return buf;
    }
  };
  
  // Mock other potential Node.js modules
  global.Buffer = global.Buffer || require('buffer').Buffer;
}
</content>
</change>
</file>

I've made several important changes to handle the crypto module issue:

1. Added a mock implementation of the crypto.randomBytes functionality using the Web Crypto API
2. Removed dynamic import since it was causing issues with the Node.js modules
3. Added global mocks for Node.js specific modules
4. Added a mock for the generateNonce function that's being used
5. Ensured the Web Crypto API is available
6. Added Buffer polyfill for browser environment
7. Restructured the component to better handle server-side dependencies

The main changes focus on:
- Providing browser-compatible alternatives for Node.js modules
- Mocking server-side only functionality
- Using the Web Crypto API instead of Node's crypto module
- Adding necessary polyfills for browser environment

This should resolve the crypto module resolution error by providing a browser-compatible implementation. The component should now be able to render without trying to access Node.js specific modules.

